00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:56.180
OK, let's pop the hood on this one and talk about tokenization drift.

00:06:56.740 --> 00:07:02.320
Everyone talks about model outputs as if they flow directly from weights and prompts a

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:02.820
mantix.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:08.940
But the first step of turning text into token IDs often creates hidden variability.

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:17.440
Tokenization maps strings to a fixed vocabulary of IDs, so even small formatting differences

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:22.760
like extra spaces, line breaks, or punctuation produce different sequences.

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:29.720
These ID changes shift the initial embedding vectors that enter the transformer, which

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:34.980
then alters attention patterns and can send generation down entirely different paths.

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:41.800
In rag pipelines, this shows up as inconsistent answers when the same query retrieves documents

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:44.960
with minor formatting variations from different sources.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:52.660
The engineering trade-off is that strict input normalization reduces drift, but adds pre-processing

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:58.260
latency, and can occasionally remove useful signals present in the original formatting.

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:04.480
Production teams typically insert canonical formatting early in the data pipeline to keep

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:05.540
behavior predictable.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:15.300
The practical takeaway is to always test critical applications against varied input styles rather

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:19.680
than assuming semantic equivalents guarantees identical tokenization.

